                                }
                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "id": "obj-31",
                                    "linecount": 53,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 577.0, 1043.0 ],
                                    "text": "================================================================================\ntransformer_flux_sat — Parameter Information\n================================================================================\n\nWHAT IT IS\nAn output transformer pushed into core saturation. The iron's flux is the\nintegral of the voltage, so a low note hits the core twice as hard as the\nnote an octave up: the lowest, loudest notes sag and break up into a farty,\nhollow growl while higher notes stay clean. No crossover filter is\ninvolved; the frequency dependence is the physics. A hysteresis loop adds\ngrit and a little drag at mid levels.\n\nSource: Macak & Schimmel, DAFx-11 (2011_05_e) §2.2, §3.2 (nonlinear output\ntransformer in a push-pull amp); Chowdhury, DAFx-19 paper 3 §2.2, §3\n(hysteresis, implicit discretization of the stiff magnetic ODE).\n\nPARAMETERS (5 + output gain)\n\n1. drive_db — 0…36 dB, default 12 dB\n   How hard the core is hit (level-neutral while unsaturated). 0 = clean;\n   24+ = heavily saturated: thin, buzzy and quieter (the collapsing core\n   eats the low end; about -45 dB RMS at 36 dB vs -32 dB at 0).\n\n2. core_size — 0.1…2, default 1\n   Saturation flux. Small = a tiny, cheap transformer that saturates early;\n   2 = big iron, mostly clean.\n\n3. hysteresis_pct — 0…100 %, default 40 %\n   Width of the core's hysteresis loop: adds odd harmonics on mid-level\n   notes and a slight lag. 0 = ideal (anhysteretic) iron.\n\n4. primary_hz — 5…200 Hz, default 30 Hz\n   Low-frequency corner set by the primary inductance (a gentle high-pass\n   below saturation). Higher = thinner, like a small transformer.\n\n5. mix — 0…1, default 1\n   Dry/wet.\n\n6. output_db — -36…+12 dB, default 0 dB\n   Final trim, last in the chain. Does not count toward the 8-parameter limit.\n\nINLETS / OUTLETS\n- in1 / in2: stereo tuba.  out1 / out2: stereo.\n- in3 / in4 are not used.\n\nNOTES\n- Measured on 0.3 sines at the defaults: THD -2 dB at 55 Hz vs -7 dB at\n  110 Hz (the low note saturates first).\n- A fixed 8 kHz lowpass (leakage inductance) rounds the steps as the core\n  comes out of saturation.\n- Implicit solver (4 Newton steps per sample): stable at any setting.\n- Pairs naturally after pushpull_crossover or triode_bias_shift.\n- No safety limiter.\n",
                                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                                }
                            }
                        ],
